---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import SectionHeader from "../components/SectionHeader.astro";
import Card from "../components/Card.astro";
import RepCard from "../components/RepCard.astro";
---
<BaseLayout
	title="Lead From The Hip — leadership development that measures behavior"
	description="Three concrete exercises for building a daily habit of leadership feedback, before you ever pick up a book, a course, or an assessment. For anyone who leads a team."
>
	<Hero
		eyebrow="Development · assessment · coaching"
		standfirst="Knowledge is cheap. Behavior is everything. Pick the one behavior that actually costs you, and get in the reps until it's habit. Not until you get it right — until you can't get it wrong."
		primaryCta={{ label: "See how it works →", href: "#method" }}
		secondaryCta={{ label: "Work with me", href: "/work-with-me" }}
	>
		Less knowing,<br />
		<span class="accent">more doing.</span>
	</Hero>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader
			title="Knowing isn't doing."
			lede="You've probably tried at least one of these. All three stop at knowing."
		/>
		<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-8">
			<Card>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-2">The book with the secret sauce.</h3>
				<p class="text-muted text-base">
					Five pillars. Ten habits. One framework for every leader — as if a weekly
					one-to-one costs an organized person and a spontaneous one the same. It
					doesn't, which is why the resolution lasted nine days.
				</p>
			</Card>
			<Card>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-2">The class that ended in applause.</h3>
				<p class="text-muted text-base">
					Ninety minutes, a good facilitator, a room that nodded along. The applause was
					real. The next day, everyone ran the same meeting the same way.
				</p>
			</Card>
			<Card>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-2">The assessment that described you accurately.</h3>
				<p class="text-muted text-base">
					It was well-built and it was right about you. You nodded. It stung a little.
					Then it went in a drawer, because a description of who you are is not an
					instruction for what to do next.
				</p>
			</Card>
		</div>
		<p class="pull-quote measure text-ink text-xl">
			Every one of those told you something. None of them built anything. What
			builds the daily habit of doing is smaller than all three combined — a
			routine, run daily, not a report read once.
		</p>
		<p class="text-ink measure mt-6">
			Books, videos, courses, assessments — none of them are the problem.
			They just can't replace an ongoing habit of seeking and acting on
			feedback. Build the habit first; everything else works better after that.
		</p>
		<p class="text-ink measure mt-4">
			Below are three exercises. Start with the first — it's the easiest way
			in — then use whichever of the other two fits what you're dealing with.
		</p>
	</section>

	<div class="bg-petrol-soft/60 border-t border-rule">
	<section id="method" class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12">
		<SectionHeader
			exercise="01"
			title="Name what's getting in your way. Then act on it."
			lede="You can't act on what you can't put your finger on. Notice it daily, then turn the patterns into a monthly intention statement."
		/>

		<p class="text-ink measure mb-6">
			Buy a physical journal and a dedicated pen that is nice to write with.
			Writing by hand slows you down just enough to actually think.
		</p>

		<div class="mb-8">
			<p class="eyebrow text-muted mb-3">Every day</p>
			<ol class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-4">
				<li class="bg-surface border border-rule rounded-[3px] p-5 flex flex-col gap-2">
					<div class="flex justify-between eyebrow text-brass">
						<span>Stage 1</span><span>Morning · 5 min</span>
					</div>
					<h3 class="text-lg">Set your intent for the day</h3>
					<p class="text-ink text-base">
						Decide what you most want to get done today and how you want to show up.
						Commit fully — and accept up front that some of it is outside your control.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li class="bg-surface border border-rule rounded-[3px] p-5 flex flex-col gap-2">
					<div class="flex justify-between eyebrow text-brass">
						<span>Stage 2</span><span>All day</span>
					</div>
					<h3 class="text-lg">Notice in the moment</h3>
					<p class="text-ink text-base">
						Watch how you read situations and make calls, especially when a strong emotion
						kicks in. When a thought starts running the show, pause and tell yourself:
					</p>
					<figure>
						<blockquote class="pull-quote text-ink text-lg">
							"This is just a thought, and not at all the thing it claims to represent."
						</blockquote>
						<figcaption class="text-muted text-sm mt-1">Adapted from Epictetus.</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</li>
				<li class="bg-surface border border-rule rounded-[3px] p-5 flex flex-col gap-2">
					<div class="flex justify-between eyebrow text-brass">
						<span>Stage 3</span><span>Evening · 5 min</span>
					</div>
					<h3 class="text-lg">End-of-day review</h3>
					<dl class="flex flex-col gap-3">
						<div>
							<dt class="eyebrow text-petrol">Start</dt>
							<dd class="text-ink text-base">
								Where did I miss a chance to lead the way I want to?
							</dd>
						</div>
						<div>
							<dt class="eyebrow text-petrol">Stop</dt>
							<dd class="text-ink text-base">
								What did I do that worked against the leader I'm trying to be?
							</dd>
						</div>
						<div>
							<dt class="eyebrow text-petrol">Continue</dt>
							<dd class="text-ink text-base">What did I do well that's worth repeating?</dd>
						</div>
					</dl>
				</li>
			</ol>
			<div class="bg-surface border border-rule rounded-[3px] p-5 flex flex-col gap-3">
				<div class="flex justify-between eyebrow text-brass">
					<span>Every week</span><span>10 min</span>
				</div>
				<p class="text-ink text-base measure">
					What theme keeps showing up in my journal this week — and what is it
					telling me?
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="bg-surface border border-rule rounded-[3px] p-5 flex flex-col gap-3 mt-4 mb-6">
				<div class="flex justify-between eyebrow text-brass">
					<span>Every month</span><span>10 min</span>
				</div>
				<p class="text-ink text-base measure">
					Turn the patterns from your weekly reviews into one intention statement:
				</p>
				<figure>
					<blockquote class="border-l-2 border-petrol pl-5">
						<p class="pull-quote text-ink text-xl md:text-2xl">
							Be "Who + What" so that "Why".
						</p>
					</blockquote>
					<figcaption class="text-muted text-sm mt-2 pl-5">
						From Ryder Carroll, author of <cite>The Bullet Journal Method</cite>.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<div class="pl-5">
					<p class="eyebrow text-muted mb-1">Example</p>
					<p class="text-ink text-base measure">
						Be a leader who lets people finish their own work so that my team grows
						faster than I can carry it.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<p class="text-muted text-base mt-4 measure">
				Miss a day? Resume — don't reconstruct it. Nothing to write? That's information
				too. Expect a pattern after six weeks, and the new behavior to feel automatic
				after three months.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader
			exercise="02"
			title="Ask for feedback. Then act on it."
			lede="Most people never find out how they come across to others. Here's how to find out, and keep checking until it changes."
		/>

		<h3 class="text-xl mb-3">Ask about one behavior, not "how am I doing"</h3>
		<div class="bg-surface border-[1.5px] border-petrol rounded-[3px] p-6 flex flex-col gap-5 mb-6">
			<p class="eyebrow text-petrol">Ask someone who actually sees it happen</p>
			<p class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-medium text-ink measure">
				"I'm working on being more concise when I present to senior leaders — I tend
				to over-explain. Would you watch for that in tomorrow's meeting and give me
				your honest read afterward?"
			</p>
			<div class="grid grid-cols-[3.5rem_1fr] gap-x-3 gap-y-1 border-t border-rule pt-4">
				<span class="eyebrow text-brass">Why</span>
				<p class="text-ink font-medium">It names the behavior, sets them up in advance, and asks for the truth.</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="mb-8">
			<p class="eyebrow text-muted mb-3">How to run it</p>
			<div class="flex flex-col">
				<div class="grid grid-cols-[2.5rem_1fr] gap-x-3 py-3 border-t border-rule">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-muted tabular-nums">01</span>
					<p class="text-ink text-base">
						<span class="font-medium">Pick one behavior.</span> Specific enough that
						people would notice it — or notice it missing. Start with a theme from
						Exercise 01, or something you already suspect.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="grid grid-cols-[2.5rem_1fr] gap-x-3 py-3 border-t border-rule">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-muted tabular-nums">02</span>
					<p class="text-ink text-base">
						<span class="font-medium">Ask three to five people, not one.</span> People
						who actually have line of sight on that behavior — not people who like you.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="grid grid-cols-[2.5rem_1fr] gap-x-3 py-3 border-t border-rule">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-muted tabular-nums">03</span>
					<p class="text-ink text-base">
						<span class="font-medium">Name it, then ask forward.</span> Tell them what
						you're watching for before it happens, not after — it's easier to observe
						than to remember.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="grid grid-cols-[2.5rem_1fr] gap-x-3 py-3 border-t border-b border-rule">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-muted tabular-nums">04</span>
					<p class="text-ink text-base">
						<span class="font-medium">Tell them what you changed.</span> It's the only
						reason they'll keep being honest with you.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<p class="pull-quote measure text-ink text-lg">
			One conversation won't change much. Neither will one week of writing. The gap
			between how you see yourself and how others describe you when you're not in the
			room only closes with repetition.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader
			exercise="03"
			title="Break the reaction down. Then act on it."
			lede="Some reactions happen faster than you can think — frustration, defensiveness, the urge to jump in. This exercise slows the moment down and replaces the reaction with one specific move."
		/>
		<p class="text-ink measure mb-6">
			The following exercise is based on Albert Ellis's work with leaders.
			He found they were rarely short on intelligence, business acumen, or
			strategy — they broke down by turning a practical problem into an
			emotional one. The rep below runs his ABC model, the core of Rational
			Emotive Behavior Therapy (REBT).
		</p>
		<RepCard />
		<p class="text-muted mt-4 measure">
			No new personality required. Just the same specific move, run the same way,
			until it holds up under pressure.
		</p>
	</section>
	</div>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader title="Who this is for" />
		<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
			<div>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-3">This works if you:</h3>
				<ul class="list-disc list-inside text-ink flex flex-col gap-2 text-base">
					<li>Have tried to change a habit before and watched it not stick</li>
					<li>Care about how you come across to people, not just what you meant to do</li>
					<li>
						Are willing to practice something small and specific, on purpose, even
						when it's inconvenient
					</li>
					<li>Would rather find out whether it worked than feel good about having tried</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-3">This doesn't work if you:</h3>
				<ul class="list-disc list-inside text-ink flex flex-col gap-2 text-base">
					<li>Want a label more than a behavior to change</li>
					<li>Are looking for a diagnosis of someone else, not yourself</li>
					<li>Want a program to roll out to a team or an organization</li>
					<li>
						Need clinical support — that's a different profession, and I'll refer you
					</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader
			title="This is what doing it looks like."
			lede="Most weeks, I'll post how the exercises went for me — what a colleague said, what showed up in my journal, what I changed. Eventually, other people's too. Reading about it isn't the work; watching someone do it is the next best thing."
		/>
		<a
			href="https://substack.com/@leadfromthehip"
			class="inline-block bg-petrol text-paper px-5 py-3 rounded-[3px] font-medium hover:opacity-90 focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-petrol"
		>
			Follow on Substack →
		</a>
	</section>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader
			title="If you want more"
			lede="Most of this is free. Occasionally I take on a client who wants the full version: one problem at work, worked for ninety days, with numbers at both ends."
		/>
		<a
			href="/work-with-me"
			class="inline-block bg-petrol text-paper px-5 py-3 rounded-[3px] font-medium hover:opacity-90 focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-petrol"
		>
			Work with me →
		</a>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
